Exactly. Once you stop treating “a story” as merely an account of events and instead treat it as an information-control structure, cover and concealment become two members of a much larger family.

A cover story answers: “What should they believe instead?”
A concealment story answers: “What should they fail to notice?”

From there, you can derive several other narrative primitives:

* Camouflage story — makes the important thing resemble ordinary background. It does not necessarily hide the object; it destroys its salience.
* Decoy story — creates something more noticeable than the thing you care about. Attention gets captured elsewhere.
* Misdirection story — the facts may all be visible, but the observer is encouraged to connect the wrong dots.
* Framing story — controls the interpretation rather than the observations. Same evidence, different meaning.
* Pretext story — supplies an acceptable reason for an action whose real motivation lies elsewhere.
* Bridge story — explains the transition between two states so an otherwise suspicious discontinuity feels natural.
* Continuity story — makes change appear to be persistence: “nothing important has changed.”
* Transformation story — does the inverse: presents continuity as a meaningful break or reinvention.
* Compartment story — gives each audience only the narrative appropriate to its local slice of reality. Nobody necessarily possesses the whole model.
* Deniability story — preserves multiple plausible interpretations so no single interpretation becomes compulsory.
* Sacrificial story — deliberately exposes a smaller explanation, error, or secret so inquiry terminates before reaching something deeper.
* Inoculation story — introduces a weakened version of an accusation, possibility, or interpretation beforehand so later exposure has less impact.
* Normalization story — repeatedly surrounds something unusual with ordinary context until it stops generating an anomaly signal.
* Permission story — changes what someone believes they are allowed to do. “People like us can do this.”
* Constraint story — creates perceived impossibility: “there is no other option,” even when alternatives technically exist.
* Coordination story — may be only partially true, but gets many independent actors behaving coherently because they share the same model.
* Identity story — instead of explaining an event, it defines the actor: hero, victim, professional, rebel, caretaker, outsider. Subsequent observations get interpreted through that identity.
* Origin story — controls how something is understood by controlling where observers believe the causal chain begins.
* Destination story — controls action by defining where the causal chain is supposedly going.
* Compression story — reduces a complicated system to something memorable enough to transmit. Myth, slogan, doctrine, brand, proverb.
* Expansion story — takes a tiny observation and embeds it in a larger causal world so people can reason beyond what they directly observed.
* Null story — “nothing happened.” Surprisingly powerful because sometimes the strongest narrative intervention is convincing the observer that no explanation is required.

And there is an important deeper distinction:

Cover changes the proposed reality.
Concealment changes visibility.
Camouflage changes contrast.
Misdirection changes attention.
Framing changes interpretation.
Pretext changes apparent causality.
Identity changes the interpreter.
Coordination changes collective behavior.

So the generalized object isn’t really a “story.”

It is something like a Narrative Operator:

\text{observations}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{belief}
\rightarrow
\text{expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

A narrative can intervene at any junction in that chain.

And that gives us an especially useful Pantheon abstraction: instead of asking “What story is being told?”, ask:

What variable is this story operating on?

Visibility? Salience? Causality? Identity? Time? Possibility? Legitimacy? Memory? Expectation? Attention? Coordination?

That turns stories from prose into something closer to an instruction set for perception and action.